\section{Find the appropriate calculation}{

Follow the help link for arguments, equations, returned fields, examples
and references. These are complementary analyses; running every row is not
a requirement for every assessment.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Can my ratings support the intended comparison?} Retained ratings, category use,
exposure and design/identification checks. Connectedness alone does not establish
precision. \code{\link[=describe_mfrm_data]{describe_mfrm_data()}}, \code{\link[=subset_connectivity_report]{subset_connectivity_report()}} and \code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}};
Linacre (1989).
\item \strong{How do ability, severity and category use explain scores?} Ordered-category
response probabilities with specified facet effects. RSM shares steps; PCM assigns
steps to one selected facet; GPCM additionally allows positive slopes. \code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}}
and \code{\link[=gpcm_capability_matrix]{gpcm_capability_matrix()}}; Andrich (1978), Masters (1982), Muraki (1992).
\item \strong{How are the parameters estimated?} MML integrates over a declared ability
distribution; JML estimates Person parameters jointly with structural parameters.
Native RSM/PCM CML conditions on each Person's total, eliminating ability
without estimating a population distribution. It supplies structural
conditional-information intervals and no ability population SD. Explicit
comparison-only CML also supports identified interaction contrasts when
other structural directions are unidentified; unavailable targets stay missing.
These routes have different assumptions and uncertainty targets. The estimator and population
sections of \code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}}; Bock and Aitkin (1981), and the JML references on that
page.
\item \strong{How uncertain is a measure or difference?} A model-specific SE or interval for a
named target, conditional on specified assumptions. A difference may need the
covariance between estimates. \code{\link[=mfrmr_interval_guide]{mfrmr_interval_guide()}}, \code{\link[=mfrm_facet_intervals]{mfrm_facet_intervals()}} and
\code{\link[=mfrm_population_intervals]{mfrm_population_intervals()}}; each page describes its calculation and limitations.
\item \strong{Which ratings are unexpected?} Infit/Outfit and residual summaries compare
ratings with model predictions; ZSTD transforms fit using the selected reference
convention. \code{\link[=diagnose_mfrm]{diagnose_mfrm()}} and \code{\link[=fit_measures_table]{fit_measures_table()}}; Wright and Masters (1982),
Linacre (2002). Extended models use \code{\link[=mfrm_response_diagnostics]{mfrm_response_diagnostics()}}.
\item \strong{Are categories functioning as intended?} Usage counts, owner-specific step
estimates and probability curves describe category use and transitions.
\code{\link[=rating_scale_table]{rating_scale_table()}}, \code{\link[=category_structure_report]{category_structure_report()}} and
\code{\link[=category_curves_report]{category_curves_report()}}; Andrich (1978), Masters (1982).
RSM has a common step set; PCM/GPCM has one declared step-owning facet,
which can differ from the single slope-owning facet in GPCM. Review
\code{owner_category_table} before interpreting pooled counts. Stored step
SEs describe centered offsets, not full crossing, gap or curve uncertainty.
Disorder does not automatically invalidate the model or justify collapsing
categories. Two-family curves use their separate fitted-curve route with
unavailable curve/step intervals; these ordinary helpers do not extend to it.
\item \strong{Is a rater unusually strict in a particular context?} Conditional bias screens
describe departures from the additive model for a combination of facet levels.
\code{\link[=estimate_bias]{estimate_bias()}} and \code{\link[=estimate_all_bias]{estimate_all_bias()}}; Linacre (1989) and the FACETS sources
in their help. These screens hold calibration fixed.
Use \code{\link[=bias_interaction_report]{bias_interaction_report()}} to keep the chosen review rules with saved
tables and plots. Its scatter compares observed-minus-expected score
averages with conditional logit shifts; neither its normal/profile bounds
nor repeated views provide a calibrated fairness test.
\item \strong{Are group differences specific to a facet?} DFF screens and separately matched
MH/ordinal-logistic analyses use different adjustment models and sampling
assumptions. \code{\link[=analyze_dff]{analyze_dff()}}, \code{\link[=mantel_haenszel_table]{mantel_haenszel_table()}} and
\code{\link[=ordinal_logistic_table]{ordinal_logistic_table()}}; see their References and matching rules.
\item \strong{How well are measures separated relative to error?} Separation, reliability and
strata summarize estimated spread and measurement error for a facet. The
interpretation and References sections of \code{\link[=diagnose_mfrm]{diagnose_mfrm()}} and
\link[=plot.mfrm_fit]{plot(x, type = "reliability")}; Wright and Masters (1982).
\item \strong{Do raters or scoring procedures agree?} Matched observed scores supply agreement
coefficients, mean differences or ICCs. These do not estimate MFRM severity.
\code{\link[=pairwise_kappa_table]{pairwise_kappa_table()}}, \code{\link[=gwet_ac2_table]{gwet_ac2_table()}}, \code{\link[=krippendorff_alpha_table]{krippendorff_alpha_table()}},
\code{\link[=pairwise_icc_table]{pairwise_icc_table()}} and \code{\link[=score_comparison_table]{score_comparison_table()}}; each has method-specific
References.
\item \strong{Do unexplained scores vary together?} Residual association concerns
departures from the fitted model, not agreement of the original scores.
\code{\link[=q3_statistic]{q3_statistic()}} uses standardized residuals averaged within Person-by-level
cells; it differs from Yen's raw-residual Q3. Its help identifies the
original definition and the limits of the package's screening cutoffs.
For organized residual patterns, \code{\link[=analyze_residual_pca]{analyze_residual_pca()}} distinguishes
overall combined-facet columns from separate facet-level matrices.
Eigenvalues across these different matrices do not rank facet importance.
Its reference comparisons are exploratory; neither component counts nor
fixed cutoffs establish a number of substantive abilities. Save the PCA
result before repeated plots so the reference is not generated again.
\item \strong{Do raters match an expert standard or show centrality?} Agreement with supplied
references and concentration of category use are separate rater properties.
\code{\link[=fit_rater_accuracy]{fit_rater_accuracy()}} and \code{\link[=rater_centrality_table]{rater_centrality_table()}}; their References identify the
accuracy and centrality methods and current restrictions.
Centrality's RSM residual correlation, supporting fit and rater-owned PCM
threshold SD are indirect indices with separate availability and source
status, not an estimated centrality parameter or a severity ranking.
\code{plot(fit, type = "rater_severity", diagnostics = diagnostics)} instead
orders facet locations and returns SE sources and interval-use labels.
Its whiskers are normal approximations, not likelihood-profile intervals
or pairwise tests. See \code{\link[=plot_rater_severity_profile]{plot_rater_severity_profile()}} for interpretation
and \link{mfrmr_plot_migration} for the public \code{plot()} entry point.
\code{\link[=rater_halo_network_analysis]{rater_halo_network_analysis()}} instead compares observed-score profiles
across shared contexts. Its graph centrality is a network property;
neither its edge filters nor high same-rater correlations establish
causal halo or residual independence. See the rater-effects section below
for the distinction from centrality and the horn effect.
\item \strong{What does a cut score imply?} Posterior ability probabilities concern a Person
now; classification accuracy/consistency concerns a specified future rating plan and
population. \code{\link[=score_mfrm_persons]{score_mfrm_persons()}}, \code{\link[=predict_mfrm_units]{predict_mfrm_units()}} and
\code{\link[=classification_table]{classification_table()}}; the latter gives Rudner (2001, 2005) and Lee (2010), with
DOI links.
\item \strong{Would another assessment plan be more dependable?} G-studies estimate
observed-score variance/covariance components; D-studies project specified future
plans and score weights. \code{\link[=mfrm_g_study]{mfrm_g_study()}} and
\code{\link[=mfrm_d_study]{mfrm_d_study()}}; Brennan (2001) and the cited design-specific methods.
These are separate from MFRM separation reliability.
\item \strong{How do I retain the analysis and its caveats?} Saved results, figures, notes and
exports preserve the calculation and its interpretation. \code{\link[=mfrm_results]{mfrm_results()}},
\code{\link[=mfrm_report]{mfrm_report()}}, \code{\link[=plot_data]{plot_data()}} and \code{\link[=export_mfrm_results]{export_mfrm_results()}}. See
\link{mfrm_calibration_workflow} for eligible calibration reuse.
Saving an analysis supports later review; a frozen calibration supports
scoring new Persons at known facet levels; saving a score result supports
later tables and plots without rescoring. These are different objects.
Freezing makes a checked calibration available for scoring. It does not
establish that the model or its prior suits another cohort. The calibration help links
Bock and Mislevy (1982) to conditional EAP scoring, separately from
calibration-estimation and interval-performance claims.
}
