\section{Use results as evidence about a construct}{

A construct is the attribute a score is intended to represent, such as
speaking proficiency. Begin with a claim about that score and its use:
"Content and Organization describe related aspects of performance across
the specified tasks and raters." State the population, rubric, score units,
intended composite or separate scores, and an expected pattern before
inspecting results. A different claim, such as diagnosing an individual's
weakness in Organization, needs evidence about that specific score or
difference, not just the dependability of a total.

The \emph{Standards} (AERA, APA, & NCME, 2014, Standards 1.13--1.14) require
evidence for the proposed internal structure, subscore interpretation and
composite rationale. Kane (2013) connects score-use claims to their
assumptions and supporting evidence. The following is a guide to applying
those principles with mfrmr, not a validity test implemented by the package.\tabular{lll}{
   Proposed claim \tab Inspect with mfrmr \tab Alternative explanation and warranted conclusion \cr
   Related criterion scores reflect shared differences between people. \tab G-study Person covariance, its uncertainty, and the other source matrices; \code{\link[=plot.mfrm_multivariate_gstudy]{plot.mfrm_multivariate_gstudy()}}. \tab Positive Person covariance is compatible with the claim under the model. It does not identify one latent dimension. A near-zero or negative estimate with a broad interval leaves the proposed relation unresolved, rather than establishing distinct constructs. \cr
   The association is not solely a common scoring condition. \tab Rater, Task, Person-by-Rater and Person-by-Task covariances alongside Person covariance. \tab Rater covariance describes judges shifting both scores together; task covariance describes tasks shifting both. Interactions describe person-specific shifts. Shared rubric interpretation, genuine performance demands and impressions can compete as explanations. No covariance sign identifies halo or horn. \cr
   The rubric and model represent the intended ordering. \tab Owner-specific category counts/curves, Wright-map targeting, and matching Infit/Outfit or residual diagnostics; \code{\link[=rating_scale_table]{rating_scale_table()}}, \code{\link[=plot.mfrm_fit]{plot.mfrm_fit()}}. \tab Sparse categories, poor targeting or unexplained residuals can challenge the proposed measurement. Good fit alone does not establish the meaning of the trait; an unexpected pattern calls for inspection of the rubric and performances before recoding or excluding ratings. \cr
   A proposed score is dependable enough for its stated decision. \tab G/Phi and SEM for the prespecified score, population and future plan; \code{\link[=summary.mfrm_multivariate_d_study]{summary.mfrm_multivariate_d_study()}}. \tab A heterogeneous Person population can raise a coefficient without reducing error in score units. High G/Phi concerns the declared replication, not construct validity. High MFRM rater separation means distinguishable severities, not agreement or evidence against shared scoring bias. \cr
}


\strong{Work through conflicting results.} If G is high but Person covariance
between the criteria is uncertain, the composite may be dependable while
the proposed shared relation remains unresolved. If Person covariance is
positive but Person-by-Task variation is substantial, consider whether the
intended generalization spans those tasks. A task-specific strength may be
substantive; it is error only relative to the chosen across-task target.
If fit looks satisfactory but the claimed proficiency is poorly represented
by the tasks, fit does not repair content underrepresentation.

Inspect all components rather than choosing favorable ones. Covariances
depend on units, and a raw component is not its contribution to a future
average: D-studies apply the relevant count divisors. Review negative or
indefinite component estimates and interval qualifications. Separate
pointwise intervals do not test equality between components. An interval
containing zero neither proves independence nor establishes equivalence.

\strong{Choose reporting and follow-up from the claim.} Prespecify composite
weights for substantive reasons; optimizing G/Phi after inspecting the data
can change the score being interpreted. Dependable subscores do not by
themselves establish dependable or useful differences between them. MFRM
and multivariate G-theory provide complementary evidence, not a joint
fitted model or a latent-dimensionality test. Agreement between outputs
based on the same ratings is not independent replication.

Report the claim, design, observed pattern with uncertainty, competing
explanation, justified conclusion and unresolved evidence. Where the claim
requires it, examine task content, how raters reach judgments, or relations
with an appropriate external measure. These require substantive evidence
beyond the returned tables; the package does not certify validity or
prescribe another analysis for every assessment. See the
\href{../articles/mfrmr-gtheory.html#examine-a-construct-claim}{G-theory worked example}
and the \href{../articles/mfrmr-visual-diagnostics.html#connect-a-figure-to-a-claim}{figure reading guide}.
}

\section{Choose an agreement target and keep its denominator}{

Start with the same rated response, identified by all relevant keys such as
Person, Task and Criterion. Matching several responses from one Person does
not make them independent sampling units. The runnable
\code{vignette("mfrmr-rating-agreement", package = "mfrmr")} shows matching,
exclusions, interpretation and saving without fitting a measurement model.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Identical scores and model expectations:} \code{\link[=interrater_agreement_table]{interrater_agreement_table()}}
reports exact agreement on matched contexts. Its \code{ExpectedExact} averages
products of fitted category probabilities, with calibration fixed. This
is a conditional model expectation, not the chance-agreement term in kappa
or AC2. MML uses a plug-in EAP, not posterior integration. Unavailable
primary estimates withhold expectations with an \code{ExpectedReason};
observed agreement is retained. Overall exact agreement counts every matched pair-context
opportunity; a context rated by more judges supplies more pairs.
\item \strong{Category agreement for two raters:} \code{\link[=pairwise_kappa_table]{pairwise_kappa_table()}} uses the
same common complete units for both rater margins. QWK penalizes category
differences quadratically. Numeric categories use their actual spacing;
character categories use their declared positions. Each pair may use a
different sample, so these coefficients are not a panel average.
\item \strong{Pooled agreement:} \code{\link[=krippendorff_alpha_table]{krippendorff_alpha_table()}} excludes singleton
units from both disagreements and weights pairable units by rating count.
Its ordinal distances depend on pairable-category frequencies.
\code{\link[=gwet_ac2_table]{gwet_ac2_table()}} weights pairable units equally for observed agreement;
singletons enter its expected-agreement margins. Its declared unused
categories also matter. Choose the definition before seeing which value
is larger; neither numerical missing-data convention corrects selective
assignment or missingness.
\item \strong{Reliability on an interval score scale:} \code{\link[=pairwise_icc_table]{pairwise_icc_table()}} requires
choices about selected versus randomly sampled raters, absolute agreement
versus consistency, and one rating versus the mean of two. Consistency
excludes systematic rater offsets; agreement includes them. The default
concerns a single rating from selected raters. An average-rating pairwise
ICC concerns two ratings, not the whole panel; numeric category codes
alone do not justify an interval scale.
\item \strong{A score source against a supplied reference:} \code{\link[=score_comparison_table]{score_comparison_table()}}
separates QWK, correlation and standardized mean difference. Adding a
second reference uses common complete records for all statistics and can
change the sample. A chosen reference is not automatically correct, and
the optional Williamson et al. (2012) screen is an application-specific
choice rather than a universal quality standard.
}

The raw-score helpers above return descriptive point estimates with their
counts, cases, settings and notes; they do not provide sampling intervals.
Save their \strong{complete lists} with \code{\link[base:saveRDS]{base::saveRDS()}} and export chosen tables
with \code{\link[utils:write.csv]{utils::write.csv()}}. They are not fitted-model result bundles for
\code{\link[=export_mfrm_results]{export_mfrm_results()}}, and their custom tutorial figures are not new
\code{plot()} methods. The separate \code{mfrm_interrater} bundle supports \code{summary()}
and \code{plot()}; see \code{\link[=interrater_agreement_table]{interrater_agreement_table()}} for its different repeated-
cell handling. Low residual association does not establish raw agreement,
and high agreement does not establish accuracy against an expert standard.
}

\section{Distinguish rater effects before choosing a diagnostic}{

Consider a judge scoring content, organization and language on a four-category
rubric. These questions concern different features of the ratings:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Severity:} does the judge generally give lower scores after accounting
for the modeled Person and criterion effects? Review fitted Rater locations.
\item \strong{Centrality:} does the judge compress ratings toward middle categories?
\code{\link[=rater_centrality_table]{rater_centrality_table()}} combines the supplied RSM residual/fit and
rater-specific PCM threshold descriptions. A severity near zero does not
answer this question; neither does graph-theoretic centrality.
\item \strong{Halo or horn:} does a favorable or unfavorable impression spill over
into ratings on other criteria? For example, a poor impression of language
could lower content and organization ratings too. The latter pattern is
called a horn effect; it is not defined by a negative criterion correlation.
\code{\link[=rater_halo_network_analysis]{rater_halo_network_analysis()}} describes observed-score associations and
cannot determine their psychological cause or classify halo versus horn.
Genuine relationships among performance criteria also produce correlations.
\item \strong{Local dependence:} do ratings share variation beyond the chosen model?
A supported \code{\link[=fit_mfrm_testlet]{fit_mfrm_testlet()}} model can account for a declared shared
effect, but its variance does not identify halo or horn.
}

Infit/Outfit summarize squared residuals. Low values can have several causes
and do not identify which effect occurred or its positive/negative direction.
Inspect shared-rating counts, category use and the actual performances before
drawing conclusions about a rater. Agreement among several indices computed
from the same ratings is not independent confirmation.

Direct models answer a further question: how would estimated measurements
change when a specified rater effect is included in the response model?
The FM-SC severity/centrality model and the MRFM-H halo mixture models are
\strong{not implemented}. See the original references and model distinctions in
\code{\link[=rater_centrality_table]{rater_centrality_table()}} and \code{\link[=rater_halo_network_analysis]{rater_halo_network_analysis()}}. These models
require their own identifying assumptions and uncertainty evaluation;
adding GPCM slopes or a testlet variance does not implement them.
}

\section{Choose the G-theory target and uncertainty}{

Start with the rating design and the intended score, not the word
"reliability" alone. The multivariate route also accepts \strong{one score};
multiple score columns are fixed score components, not additional random
facets or latent dimensions. All methods below require explicit
\code{assumption = "normal"}, prespecified scores/weights and the documented
sampling/assignment assumptions. An incomplete source is not permission
to use a sparse future plan: current D-studies project complete balanced plans.

First choose the quantity whose uncertainty you need:\tabular{lll}{
   Question \tab Interface \tab Meaning of the uncertainty \cr
   How uncertain is a source variance or covariance? \tab \code{\link[=confint.mfrm_multivariate_gstudy]{confint.mfrm_multivariate_gstudy()}} \tab Component interval; variance units are squared score units, covariance units are products of score units. \cr
   How uncertain are the original component estimates jointly? \tab \code{\link[=vcov.mfrm_multivariate_gstudy]{vcov.mfrm_multivariate_gstudy()}} \tab Sampling covariance and SEs of ANOVA/MINQUE components, including identifiable incomplete sources. Not covariance between individual ratings. \cr
   How uncertain is a future plan's G, Phi or SEM? \tab \code{\link[=confint.mfrm_multivariate_d_study]{confint.mfrm_multivariate_d_study()}} \tab One selected population coefficient or measurement-error target at prespecified counts. Coefficient SE and measurement SEM are different quantities. \cr
   How uncertain is the change between two plans? \tab \code{confint(d, reference = 1, ...)} or \code{\link[=mfrm_multivariate_d_compare]{mfrm_multivariate_d_compare()}} \tab Paired comparison using the shared source; never subtraction of separate marginal endpoints. \cr
}


Then select a method allowed by the \strong{observed source}. Here \code{g} is a
G-study and \code{d} its D-study. The table describes available calculations,
not a ranking by interval width or a guarantee of nominal coverage.\tabular{lll}{
   Target and source \tab Default or explicit choices \tab Qualification \cr
   Component variance, balanced ANOVA \tab \code{confint(g)}: \code{auto}/\code{mls} \tab Exact residual chi-square; other component MLS intervals are approximate. \cr
   Signed covariance between two scores, balanced ANOVA \tab Explicit \code{gci} or \code{regression_region} \tab Generalized approximate coverage or conservative enclosure; no automatic covariance default. \cr
   One-facet G/Phi, balanced ANOVA \tab \code{confint(d)}: \code{exact_f} for G, \code{gci} for Phi \tab Normal-model exact F versus generalized approximate coverage. \cr
   Two-crossed-facet G/Phi, balanced ANOVA \tab Default \code{mls}; explicit \code{delta}, \code{approximate_f}, \code{mean_square_region}, \code{reml_profile}; G also permits \code{score} \tab Distinct approximations/conservative enclosures. Read formula-completion flags for MLS; these are not interchangeable calibrations. \cr
   Nested G/Phi, balanced ANOVA \tab With unchanged source child count per parent, the one-facet defaults apply. Otherwise explicitly select \code{delta}, \code{approximate_f}, \code{mean_square_region} or \code{reml_profile}. \tab Both facets remain random. The default reduction is unavailable when the child count changes. \cr
   Relative/absolute SEM, balanced crossed/nested ANOVA \tab Default \code{mls}; two-facet sources also permit \code{mean_square_region} \tab Error-variance intervals transformed by square root; exactness depends on the contributing mean squares. \cr
   G/Phi, identifiable MINQUE source \tab Explicit \code{delta} or \code{reml_profile}; one-facet G also permits \code{spectral_f} \tab Delta concerns the original coefficient; REML has a separate center. Spectral F has an exact normal-model pivot with its own residual-information requirements. \cr
   Component variance/covariance or SEM, identifiable MINQUE source \tab Explicit \code{reml_profile}; \code{residual_contrast} additionally supplies residual components and one-facet RelativeSEM \tab REML is experimental; residual contrasts have an exact variance law or conservative signed covariance enclosure. Residual-only inference does not supply other components or AbsoluteSEM. \cr
}


\code{delta} is also an explicit G/Phi option for balanced one-facet sources;
\code{reml_profile} accepts every supported balanced source as well as identifiable
incomplete/unequal sources. \code{residual_contrast} and one-facet \code{spectral_f}
also accept retained complete ratings. These methods do not become an
automatic fallback when another calculation fails. MINQUE requests require
an explicit interval method even when the source happens to be balanced.
For derivations, required inputs, optional dependencies and references, use
\code{\link[=confint.mfrm_multivariate_gstudy]{confint.mfrm_multivariate_gstudy()}} and
\code{\link[=confint.mfrm_multivariate_d_study]{confint.mfrm_multivariate_d_study()}}. Almehrizi's (2021) coefficient-SE
formula and the limits of its extension are in the latter's \emph{Delta coefficient
SEs and intervals} section; SE accuracy does not establish Wald coverage.

For \strong{paired plans}, choose the relevant source law separately:
\itemize{
\item Balanced one-facet G/Phi map the whole common F/generalized set. The nested
G/Phi reduction requires every future child count to equal the source count.
One-facet SEM and nested SEM with a common future child count map one
error-SD interval; that future child count may differ from the source.
\item General balanced two-facet comparisons have explicit \code{mean_square_region}
enclosures from one shared source region. These retain dependence but can
be wider than the tight difference image. Two crossed random facets also
permit \code{\link[=mfrm_multivariate_d_compare]{mfrm_multivariate_d_compare()}} for a separately qualified paired
delta approximation, including identifiable MINQUE sources.
\item Explicit \code{reml_profile} supplies G/Phi/SEM comparisons for supported
complete or incomplete sources. Proportional one-facet/common-child nested
plans map a common profile; other plans profile the difference directly.
Calibration remains approximate, especially at zero-contrast singularities.
\item For retained one-facet ratings, \code{spectral_f} G and \code{residual_contrast}
RelativeSEM differences map their shared confidence sets. Marginal
\code{method = "delta"} in \code{confint(d)} does not supply paired differences.
}

\strong{Read and save the result.} \code{\link[=summary.mfrm_multivariate_d_study]{summary.mfrm_multivariate_d_study()}} with
\code{intervals = TRUE} collects supported methods, original points, any separate
\code{IntervalCenter}, source/future counts and weights. Select \code{parm} explicitly
when the chosen method supports only some metrics. Ordinary \code{summary(d)}
returns the coefficient table. \code{confint(d, method = c(...))} compares compatible
methods for one target; \code{confint(g)} selects one component method at a time.

Wide computable intervals remain results. Delta limits outside \verb{[0,1]},
negative original components, empty sets, failed numerical checks and absent
methods are different states. Read \code{Status}, availability and saved notes.
A REML or residual-projection center must not be relabeled as MINQUE.
General bootstrap intervals are not implemented. Nonnormal robustness,
selection-adjusted inference and sparse/unequal future plans are not supplied.
These limits do not make an implemented interval calculation nonexistent.

There is no universal acceptable width or G/Phi cutoff. Define the intended
score use, a meaningful improvement or threshold, and task/rater costs before
choosing a plan. An interval spanning a decision threshold describes
uncertainty about that decision; selecting the narrowest method after seeing
its result does not resolve it. A method comparison on one dataset is
sensitivity analysis, not a coverage study. Neither high G/Phi nor a narrow
interval alone establishes construct validity or fairness.

Use \code{plot(d)} for point curves, \code{intervals = TRUE} for an explicit interval
request, or supply saved intervals for reuse. \code{plot(ci)} compares D-study
intervals on one axis; \code{plot(g, intervals = TRUE, ...)} shows component
intervals and retains them in the returned plot object. Save complete
results with \code{saveRDS()}; \code{as_ggplot()} renders saved plot data without fitting.
Ribbons are pointwise, not simultaneous bands; overlap is not a paired test.
The \href{../articles/mfrmr-gtheory.html}{G-theory tutorial} supplies a bundled-data
reading path, and the function help holds mathematical details and references.
The simpler \code{\link[=mfrm_generalizability]{mfrm_generalizability()}} / \code{\link[=mfrm_d_study]{mfrm_d_study()}} route is a separate
main-effects approximation; its residual sensitivity is not a confidence interval.
}

\section{Read the method before interpreting the number}{

\strong{Model:} RSM/PCM use unit discrimination. One-family GPCM assigns slopes
to one selected facet; MML allows a different step owner, whereas JML
requires the same owner. The experimental two-family MML route has exactly
two non-Person facets and additional restrictions. Check
\code{\link[=gpcm_capability_matrix]{gpcm_capability_matrix()}} before transferring an ordinary-model analysis.
The original response-model papers do not establish the performance of
every mfrmr estimator, extension or interval.

\strong{Estimation and scoring:} MML uses numerical integration of the marginal
likelihood. JML includes Person parameters in the joint likelihood. EAP
scoring subsequently averages over a conditional ability distribution;
its posterior SD is not a sampling SE for a structural parameter. Ordinary
RSM/PCM MML fixes N(0,1) when \code{population_formula = NULL}; estimating a
population model is an explicit choice. A histogram of EAP estimates is
not itself an estimate of the latent population density. Numerical
integration, population assumptions and the source of uncertainty are
documented separately in \code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}} and the scoring help pages.

\strong{Fit:} Infit weights squared standardized residuals by their model
variance; Outfit gives each retained residual equal weight when observation
weights are all one. Values above one indicate more residual variation than
the reference model expects. A flag is a reason to inspect ratings and
assumptions, not a verdict on a rater. Read the selected residual basis,
weighting and ZSTD convention in \code{\link[=diagnose_mfrm]{diagnose_mfrm()}}. Posterior diagnostics
for extended models do not inherit ordinary fit cutoffs.

\strong{Reliability and strata:} the ordinary summaries adjust the spread of
finite measures using their matching squared SEs. Invalid or missing SEs
can make the summary unavailable. High rater separation indicates
distinguishable severities, not agreement; low separation can also reflect
poor precision. Strata is a hypothetical separation index, not a discovered
number of groups or a set of pass/fail boundaries. Model and fit-adjusted
summaries are not confidence bounds. See the
\href{https://www.winsteps.com/facetman64/reliability.htm}{Facets explanation}
alongside mfrmr's estimator-specific conventions in \code{\link[=diagnose_mfrm]{diagnose_mfrm()}}.
These marginal-SE summaries omit covariance among calibrated measures.
Applying variance subtraction to EAP estimates is not posterior-variance
EAP reliability, and neither quantity is a G-theory coefficient.

\strong{Post-fit shrinkage:} \code{\link[=apply_empirical_bayes_shrinkage]{apply_empirical_bayes_shrinkage()}} adds a
descriptive zero-centered normal-model adjustment while retaining the
fitted estimates. Its plug-in SD conditions on the chosen prior variance;
it is not a calibrated sampling SE or a general improvement guarantee.
Additional Person shrinkage requires ordinary JML sampling SEs. MML EAP
estimates and posterior SDs must not be fed through a second such update.

\strong{Categories and missingness:} use the intended rubric, not only the
observed score range. \code{category_policy = "preserve"} retains that ladder;
\code{"collapse"} changes a gapped ladder and records the mapping. Missing
assigned scores, unassigned ratings and unavailable rubric categories are
different situations. The current ordinary models use a common category
range; owner-specific ranges are not implied by PCM step ownership. See
\code{\link[=describe_mfrm_data]{describe_mfrm_data()}} and \code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}} for support checks and retained rows.

\strong{Uncertainty:} distinguish the estimate, its numerical status and the
interpretation allowed by its assumptions. A finite experimental interval
is not evidence of nominal coverage. A missing endpoint is not infinity.
Conditional bias screens and fixed-calibration Person intervals omit
calibration uncertainty. Use \code{\link[=mfrmr_interval_guide]{mfrmr_interval_guide()}} to select the target
before choosing a method.
}

\section{Find and cite the supporting literature}{

Open the function's \strong{Details}, named method sections and \strong{References};
report its selected settings as well as the paper. For example,
\code{help("diagnose_mfrm")} connects the Infit/Outfit definitions to the ZSTD
conventions and reliability formulas; \code{help("classification_table")}
explains how the Lee recursion differs from the Rudner approximation.
The foundational sources below identify the model/estimation framework.
Specialist help pages provide the references for their own formulas and
adaptations. A citation to a general framework is not validation of a new
implementation or of your data's assumptions.

Use \code{citation("mfrmr")} for the software citation and
\code{packageVersion("mfrmr")} for the installed version. Cite the relevant
methodological sources as well. A DOI identifies a paper even if access to
its full text requires a library subscription.
}

